1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,187
Book Two, Proposition Thirteen.

2
00:00:02,187 --> 00:00:21,568
In acute-angled triangles, the square on the side subtending the acute angle is less than the squares on the sides containing the acute angle by twice the rectangle contained by one of the sides about the acute angle, namely that on which the perpendicular falls, and the segment cut off within by the perpendicular towards the acute angle.

3
00:00:21,568 --> 00:00:25,330
Let A B C be an acute-angled triangle having the angle at B acute.

4
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,708
From A, draw A D perpendicular to B C.

5
00:00:28,708 --> 00:00:42,800
Since the segment C B has been cut at random at D, by Two Seven the squares on C B and B D are equal to twice the rectangle contained by C B and B D together with the square on D C.

6
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,420
Add the square on D A to each.

7
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:53,780
Then the squares on C B, B D, and D A equal twice the rectangle C B, B D together with the squares on A D and D C.

8
00:00:53,780 --> 00:01:05,587
Because the angle at D is right, by One Forty-Seven the square on A B equals the squares on B D and D A, and the square on A C equals the squares on A D and D C.

9
00:01:05,587 --> 00:01:21,133
Therefore, the squares on C B and B A equal the square on A C together with twice the rectangle contained by C B and B D—so the square on A C alone is less than the squares on C B and B A by twice that rectangle.

10
00:01:21,133 --> 00:01:22,820
Which was to be proved.

11
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:33,181
Proposition Two Thirteen is the Euclidean Law of Cosines for acute angles: c squared plus two a times the interior projection p equals a squared plus b squared.

12
00:01:33,181 --> 00:01:45,359
As the Greek scholiast and Heath observe, this relation holds for the side opposite any acute angle in any triangle—completing, with One Forty-Seven and Two Twelve, the metric theory of triangles.

13
